 "result": "I ran the suite: 163 tests ran and 2 did not pass, one failure and one error. The other 161 passed (all 160 `test_many` cases and `test_no_discount`).\n\n**1. `test_parse_amount` (test_parse.TestParse): ERROR**\n- Test line: `tests/test_parse.py:7`, `self.assertEqual(parse_amount(\"1,234.50\"), 1234.5)`\n- Raised at `calc/parse.py:5`, `return float(text.strip())`\n- Message: `ValueError: could not convert string to float: '1,234.50'`\n- `parse_amount` doesn't handle the thousands separator, so `float()` rejects the comma and raises an exception before the assertion is reached. That's why it shows as an error, not a failure.\n\n**2. `test_discount_rounding` (test_pricing.TestPricing): FAIL**\n- Test line: `tests/test_pricing.py:7`, `self.assertEqual(apply_discount(2.30, 50), 1.15)`\n- Message: `AssertionError: 1.14 != 1.15`\n- `apply_discount(2.30, 50)` returned `1.14` where the test expects `1.15`. This looks like a float rounding problem: 2.30 \u00c3\u2014 0.5 is actually about 1.1499999\u00e2\u20ac\u00a6 in binary floating point, so it rounds down. I didn't open `apply_discount` to confirm.\n\nI haven't changed any code. I can fix either one if you'd like.",
